  "background": "Compiled by Major M. McCulloch, Political Agent at Manipur, and published in 1859 in 'Account of the Valley of Munnipore and of the Hill Tribes' (Records of the Government of India, No. XXVII). This is the earliest known printed wordlist of the Tangkhul language, referred to under the historical ethnonym 'Luhuppa' (referring to the woven cane helmets worn by northern warriors). Preserves archaic 19th-century phonological forms.",
  "phonology_notes": "Transcribed using pre-standard 19th-century English phonetic approximations (e.g., 'mee' for mi, 'mick' for mik, 'nahlung' for ngalung, 'shem' for shim).",
